[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003448","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003448","source_record_id":"rec_003448","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"AI for Scientific Discovery in Omics Data-Driven Precision Medicine.","abstract":"In recent years, the rapid advancement of high-throughput technologies has led to the generation of vast and complex multi-omics datasets that are valuable for characterizing and understanding complex cell signaling network systems. On the other hand, large language models (LLMs), domain-specific foundation models (FMs) and AI agents, have achieved significant breakthroughs and have been revolutionizing scientific research. The convergence of these two trends is catalyzing a new era for biomedical research to augment and speed up scientific discovery and the development of precision medicine. In this study, we examine the largescale omics datasets, emerging applications, and challenges at the intersection of massive omic datasets and related AI models and agents, highlighting how their integration is reshaping the landscape of biomedical research and precision medicine.","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nIn recent years, the rapid advancement of high-throughput technologies has led to the generation of vast and complex multi-omics datasets that are valuable for characterizing and understanding complex cell signaling network systems. On the other hand, large language models (LLMs), domain-specific foundation models (FMs) and AI agents, have achieved significant breakthroughs and have been revolutionizing scientific research. The convergence of these two trends is catalyzing a new era for biomedical research to augment and speed up scientific discovery and the development of precision medicine. In this study, we examine the largescale omics datasets, emerging applications, and challenges at the intersection of massive omic datasets and related AI models and agents, highlighting how their integration is reshaping the landscape of biomedical research and precision medicine.\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nThe recent and continued advances and breakthroughs in AI foundation models, e.g., large language models (LLMs), agentic AI and AI agents, domainspecific foundation models, are revolutionizing and transforming scientific data analysis and scientific knowledge discovery. In AI4Science, AI is not just our tool, AI is our collaborator for scientific discovery. AI models are becoming comparable with human scientists in many aspects of the scientific discovery process, such as automated data retrieval, coding for data analysis, large-scale literature search and knowledge synthesis, and hypothesis or idea generation. 1-8\n\nIn life science, omics-data driven mechanism discovery and precision medicine studies represent the most fundamental and challenging barriers to both the theoretical and practical applications of AI, considering that there is a lack of effective or curative therapies for most diseases, and the incomplete understanding of the complex and large-scale biological and disease causal molecular signaling systems. In recent years, large-scale and massive omics datasets of diseases have been generated and publicly accessible to characterize and profile the dysfunctional biological processes in diseases from multi- level views measured by using multi-omics data, like genetics, epigenetics, transcriptomics, proteomics, and metabolic datasets. Then, the meeting and convergence of LLMs, AI agents, foundation models and the massive omics datasets are speeding up scientific discovery in AI for precision medicine (AI4PM). [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nAdvances in large-scale multi-omics data generation and analysis have identified numerous molecular targets and elucidated parts of the key mechanisms involved in disease pathogenesis. However, the holistic and deep understanding of complex disease cell signaling systems remains limited. Consequently, effective and curative treatments are still lacking for most complex diseases, e.g., cancers, Alzheimer's disease, systematic chronic inflammation, lysosomal storage disorder diseases. In recent years, continuous breakthroughs in generative AI and foundation models have been transforming the paradigm of data analysis and scientific research. The convergence of transformative AI models with large-scale omics data marks the beginning of a new era in biomedical research and precision medicine.\n\nThe AI for biomedical research and precision medicine (AI4PM) is still in its early stages. Unlike building LLMs and foundation models based on human understandable language and image datasets, the integration, representation, analysis, interpretation, and applications of complex multiomics data using novel AI models represent some of the most challenging tasks in both AI and biomedical research/precision medicine. There are several fundamental challenges, e.g., problem definition and alignment (to map diverse and often vague, user-specific questions to appropriate, well-defined formulations and prompts that can effectively guide AI systems in subsequent analysis planning and task execution); [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Massive Omics Datasets Are Publicly Accessible]\nAlong with the advancement of next generation sequencing (NGS) technology, massive omics datasets of diverse diseases have been generated and publicly accessible. These large-scale omics datasets are valuable and essential for the development of novel and transformative AI models in the field of life science and precision medicine to understand pathogenesis and mechanisms of diseases, identifying novel therapeutic targets to guide the development of effective medications in precision medicine. However, it remains a challenging task and open problem to integrate and interpret the complex and heterogeneous massive datasets to mine knowledge embedded in these datasets. Though the success of foundation models (FMs) in language and computer vision, novel and transformative AI foundation models are urgently needed for analyzing and mining these datasets to mine the key disease targets, signaling pathways and effective medications for diverse diseases.\n\n[data_representation: Tissue-Level Omics Datasets]\nAmong diseases, cancer is one of the leading causes of death worldwide. To investigate the complex pathogenesis and molecular mechanisms of cancer for the development of precision medications, large-scale omics datasets have been generated. For example, in The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) project, 10,11 multi-omics, i.e., genetic, epigenetic, transcriptomic, and proteomic data of a large number of cancer samples from about 33 cancer types from different organs have been generated, which are accessible via the Genomic Data Commons Data Portal. 12 Analysis of these valuable datasets have identified numerous cancer specific targets and pathways. 10 In addition, the complete set of multi-omics data of 1,000 cancer cell lines were generated in the Cancer Cell Line Encyclopedia (CCLE) project. 13 Different from human omics data, the omics data of cancer cell lines can be paired with rich and massive perturbation data of these cancer cell lines. The integration of multi-omics data and massive perturbation data are valuable to uncover the vulnerable targets and understand the signaling pathways and molecular mechanisms of cancer cells, as well as drug response to given drugs and drug combinations, which are publicly accessible via cancer dependency map (DepMap) project. 14,15 To understand the mode of action (MoA) of drugs, the connectivity map (CMAP) project generated the gene signatures of thousands of drugs/compounds on a set of cancer cells, 16-18 which are widely used in drug prediction and repositioning. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Single Cell-Level Omics Datasets]\nCompared with tissue-level omics data that measures the average signals across heterogeneous cell populations, single cell omics data is necessary to identify disease specific cell-types or cellsubtypes, cell-specific signaling system patterns, and investigate signaling communications within disease MEs. 24,25 Cells are the basic structural and functional units of the human body. Though they share the same genetic blueprint, the process of differentiation transforms them into various cell types and subtypes with distinct functions, 6 which form tissues, organs, and organ systems, creating an organized and coordinated body structure.\n\nFigure 2. UMAP visualization of a sample set of immune cells. (a) Global UMAP showing macrophages, fibroblasts, B cells, and T cells. (b-e) UMAP projections of each cell type individually, illustrating distinct transcriptional clustering within macrophages, fibroblasts, B cells, and T cells.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nIn diseases, the dysfunction of specific cells or subpopulations will alter the healthy body or organ cellular architectures and signaling systems. For example, the immune and inflammatory signaling processes within disease microenvironments (MEs) or niche, with the signaling communications between the activated immune cells and damaged or dysfunctional non-immune cells, play important roles in many diseases. 27 The systematic chronic inflammation (SCI) is believed as one of causal factors of 50% of all deaths. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":3,"line_start":11,"line_end":14,"text":"In recent years, the rapid advancement of high-throughput technologies has led to the generation of vast and complex multi-omics datasets that are valuable for characterizing and understanding complex cell signaling network systems. On the other hand, large language models (LLMs), domain-specific foundation models (FMs) and AI agents, have achieved significant breakthroughs and have been revolutionizing scientific research. The convergence of these two tren